\title{[D3] The Roving Photographer (2 pts)}
\date{2026-10-09T17:31:54Z}
\tag{algorithms}
\tag{problem}

\import{algorithms-macros}

\p{Phoebe the Photographer is just leaving for work at the small-town
newspaper where she works as a staff photographer, when she suddenly
remembers that she was supposed to take a photograph to decorate page
C17.  She will just have to take a photograph on the way, but she is
worried about getting to work late!}

\p{Phoebe's town can be represented by a directed, weighted graph with
#{V} vertices and #{E} edges, where the weight on each edge represents
the time needed to travel along that street in the given direction.
(As usual, two-way streets are represented by one edge in each
direction, possibly with different weights.)  One particular vertex
represents Pheobe's house, and another vertex represents her work.
There are various locations (\ie vertices) at which Pheobe could take
a photograph; each such vertex has a number representing the amount of
time Pheobe would need to spend taking a photograph there (some
photographs can be taken quickly, while others need a while to set
up).}

\p{Pheobe needs to travel to some location where she can take a
photograph, pause long enough to take the photo, and then travel to
her workplace.  Explain how Pheobe could compute such a path which is
as short as possible, and analyze the running time of your algorithm.}

\hint{You may come up with a #{\Theta(VE\lg V)} algorithm, but it is
possible to do better: it can be done in #{\Theta(E \lg V)} time.}

\hint{How can you transform the graph so that the problem is solved by
a single call to Dijkstra's Algorithm?}

\hint{Consider duplicating each vertex, one to represent visiting that
location with no photograph taken, and another to represent visiting
that location with a photograph already taken.}
